40,2,1,talk,Rin,,"Wow, I found out that setting up desks can take a long time! Some people spend almost ten minutes just getting started. If we change things too often, that's a lot of wasted time!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The financial data is concerning. We are billed by area regardless of headcount, and every single desk move incurs a significant cost. We cannot afford a layout that requires frequent adjustments or expensive rewiring.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"My findings suggest that while proximity boosts immediate communication and efficiency, unplanned changes are incredibly disruptive. We need a layout that balances this synergy with a stability that prevents chaotic, last-minute shuffles.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Shion,,"The data confirms my suspicions. Noise levels are inconsistent across the room, and more importantly, ergonomic setups are fragile. It takes days of fine-tuning to avoid physical strain. Any plan involving frequent moves is logically unsound.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Yumi,,It's so interesting! I found out that our new teammates learned so much just by overhearing us. We should definitely keep people close together so everyone can learn and feel included!,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Rin,,"But if we're always moving to be ""close,"" we'll spend all our time plugging in cables! I don't want our mornings to be a big scramble. We need something that stays put!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"Exactly. The cost of re-running power for every change is a recurring drain on a budget that hasn't grown. We must prioritize a stable, long-term arrangement to avoid these unnecessary expenses.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"Stability is important, but we must be honest: some seats are naturally worse than others. If we commit to a long-term layout, we need a fair way to decide who takes the less desirable spots.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Shion,,"Fairness isn't about rotating through bad seats; it's about optimizing the environment. If we move constantly, we destroy the ergonomic precision required for health. Stability isn't just a budget preference; it's a physical necessity.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Yumi,,"I see what you mean, but we can't forget about the sunlight! The survey showed people really miss the windows. If we stay put, we have to make sure the layout is fair and bright for everyone!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Rin,,"I agree! But if we move around to share the sun, we'll waste those precious minutes every morning. Let's find a way to be fair without making us scramble with cables!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"The most logical solution is to choose a layout that minimizes the need for rewiring. We should avoid any plan that suggests flexibility through frequent movement, as the costs are simply too high.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"If we prioritize stability to save costs and avoid disruption, we risk creating a permanent hierarchy of seating. We should consider a rotation system or a clear agreement on how to distribute the less ideal spots.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Shion,,"A rotation system is a disaster. Forcing us to reset our ergonomic adjustments every few weeks is an inefficient waste of time and a risk to our health. We need fixed positions, not a game of musical chairs.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Yumi,,"I understand, but we can't just ignore the social side! If we're fixed in place, we should group people by role so the new joiners can keep learning from us naturally. Let's be stable but smart!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Rin,,"I'm with Shion and Toshio on this! Let's pick a layout that stays put. I don't want to waste my morning fighting with cables just to be ""flexible."" Stability is the way to go!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"I concur. Given the fixed billing and the cost per desk move, any plan that encourages frequent changes is financially irresponsible. We must commit to a single, stable layout to protect our budget.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I understand the need for stability, but we cannot ignore the human cost of a permanent ""bad seat."" If we choose a fixed layout, we must explicitly agree on how to handle the less desirable spots.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Shion,,"The ""human cost"" of a bad seat is negligible compared to the physical cost of chronic strain. Let's stop pretending that rotating through discomfort is a solution. We need a fixed, optimized layout and the discipline to keep it.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Yumi,,"I agree that we need stability, but let's make sure that stability doesn't mean isolation! If we're staying put, let's group ourselves so we can keep supporting each other and helping new people grow!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
